# simpl_orm integration guide

> **Maintained by**: the `simpl_orm` repo, auto-synced to simpl_knowledge.
> **Source of truth**: `simpl-techs/simpl_orm/.agent/SKILL.md`

## What this library is

`simpl_orm` is the shared async ORM layer for simpl PostgreSQL/Supabase projects. It combines SQLModel, SQLAlchemy 2.0 asyncio, asyncpg, repository factories, pagination, and RLS user context helpers.

## Installation

```bash
poetry add "simpl-orm @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_orm.git@main"
```

## Basic usage

```python
from sqlmodel import Field
from simpl_orm import BaseModel, DatabaseManager

class User(BaseModel, table=True):
    __tablename__ = "users"
    __table_args__ = {"schema": "public"}
    email: str = Field(unique=True)
    name: str

async def main() -> None:
    db = DatabaseManager()
    await db.initialize()
    factory = db.setup_factory(require_schema=True, allow_public_schema=True)
    factory.register(User)
    async with db.session_scope() as session:
        users = db.repository(User, session)
        await users.create({"email": "test@example.com", "name": "Test"})
    await db.shutdown()
```

## Choosing a driver

`DatabaseManager` talks to Postgres through two independent drivers, and they cannot share connections. In direct mode the default therefore opens two pools — an asyncpg one and a SQLAlchemy one.

Set `driver` when your process only uses one of them, and the other pool is never opened:

```python
from simpl_orm.config.settings import DatabaseSettings
from simpl_orm import DatabaseManager

# ORM-only service: session_scope()/repositories, no raw queries.
db = DatabaseManager(settings=DatabaseSettings(driver="sqlalchemy"))
```

| `driver` | What you can call |
| --- | --- |
| `both` (default) | everything |
| `sqlalchemy` | `session_scope()`, `get_session()`, repositories, `create_tables()`/`drop_tables()` |
| `asyncpg` | `acquire()`, `transaction()`, `execute()`, `fetch()`/`fetchrow()`/`fetchval()`, `executemany()` |

Calling a surface your driver does not provide raises `DriverNotEnabledError` (a subclass of `simpl_orm.exceptions.ConnectionError`) with a message naming the setting to change. `driver` is independent of `connection_mode`: pooler modes still never open a local pool, whichever driver you pick.

## Sizing the pool

Direct mode is the only mode that pools locally; the pooler modes hand a
connection per scope to Supavisor and ignore every dial below.

| Setting | Default | Applies to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pool_min_size` | 10 | asyncpg only — connections kept warm |
| `pool_max_size` | 20 | both — the ceiling, and what SQLAlchemy retains |
| `pool_timeout` | 30.0 | SQLAlchemy only — seconds a checkout waits for a free connection |

`pool_min_size` reaches asyncpg alone, because a SQLAlchemy `QueuePool` has no
floor to set: it opens connections lazily and keeps `pool_size` of them. The
engine is therefore sized from `pool_max_size` with no overflow, so the ceiling
is unchanged and every connection under it is retained rather than re-opened.

Lower `pool_timeout` when the caller has a deadline of its own — a lease to
renew, a request to answer. A checkout that waits past that deadline has
already lost the work it was waiting for, and queueing longer only deepens the
contention it is queued behind. It raises `sqlalchemy.exc.TimeoutError`, which
`session_scope()` re-raises wrapped in `TransactionError` (reachable through
`__cause__`). Treat it as backpressure and shed the work: it is a statement
about concurrency, not a transient fault, so retrying it in place adds load to
a pool that is already full.

## Rules and conventions

- Use async sessions and repositories. Do not introduce sync SQLAlchemy patterns in async services.
- Set `driver` to the single API surface your process uses; leave it at `both` only when you genuinely call both.
- Define explicit schemas with `__table_args__` unless the model is intentionally public schema.
- Use `set_rls_user_context` when queries must run under a Supabase/RLS user context.
- Use `paginate`/`Page` for reusable pagination contracts.

## Common pitfalls

- Do not create one `DatabaseManager` per request. Initialize once and reuse per process.
- Do not bypass repository/session boundaries with ad hoc connection handling in consumers.
- Do not change base model behavior without checking all repos that define SQLModel entities.

## Testing

```bash
conda activate simpl_orm
poetry install
poetry run pytest
poetry run ruff check .
```

## What this library does NOT do

- Does not define simpl business services. Use `simpl_core`.
- Does not expose FastAPI routes. Use `simpl_api`.
- Does not run workflow jobs. Use `simpl_flow`.

